  "shift_analysis": {
    "is_column_shifted": true,
    "diagnosis": "The columns are definitively shifted / bit-mask transposed. In standard network traffic, the TCP URG flag is virtually non-existent (< 0.01%, deprecated in RFC 6093), yet in Day 1 benign flows P(URG=1 | TCP) = 71.53%. Conversely, ACK is present on virtually all TCP data segments (> 95%), yet in Day 1 benign flows P(ACK=1 | TCP) = 29.05%. This inversion indicates that CICFlowMeter's byte-level bit-masking for TCP flags transposed the URG bit (0x20) with the ACK bit (0x10) or PSH-ACK combination. Furthermore, UDP and Raw IP flows correctly have 0.0000 across all flags. The earlier speculation that 'flags appear in TCP teardown' is physically incorrect and deleted."
  }
